---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { USCCB_ORDER, USCCB_LEGEND } from '../lib/ratings.js';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base title="About" description="About the USCCB / Catholic News Service movie reviews archive." active="about">
  <h1>About this archive</h1>
  <p class="lede">A historical archive — coverage runs 1905–2011 and is not updated.</p>

  <p>
    These 13,371 capsule and full-length film reviews were originally published by the
    <strong>USCCB Office for Film &amp; Broadcasting / Catholic News Service</strong>. They were
    parsed from the original archive pages into a Postgres database (hosted on Supabase), which
    is the source of truth for this site; the downloadable SQLite and CSV exports are generated
    from it. The collection spans from the early silent era through 2011, when the archive ends.
    This site is an independent reference copy and is not affiliated with or endorsed by the
    USCCB; review text remains © its original publishers.
  </p>

  <h2>USCCB rating legend</h2>
  <dl class="dl-list">
    {USCCB_ORDER.map((r) => (
      <>
        <dt><span class="chip usccb">{r}</span></dt>
        <dd>{USCCB_LEGEND[r]}{r === 'A-IV' && ' (older designation, later folded into L)'}</dd>
      </>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <h2>Using the query page</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={`${base}query`}>Query</a> page lets you run read-only SQL directly against the
    live archive database. Queries are a single <code>SELECT</code>/<code>WITH</code>, run with a
    5-second timeout, and return up to 1,000 rows. A few starting points:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Counts by USCCB rating: <code>select usccb_code, count(*) from movies group by 1;</code></li>
    <li>Full-text search via the <code>search_tsv</code> column: <code>where search_tsv @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english', '…')</code> rather than <code>like '%…%'</code>.</li>
    <li>The <code>movies</code> table is all real films; the 322 "see other title" alias stubs live in a separate <code>redirects</code> table.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Spotted a film that's missing or mis-rated? You can <a href={`${base}submit`}>suggest one</a> —
    submissions are reviewed by a moderator before anything is added.
  </p>

  <h2>Data &amp; API</h2>
  <p>
    The whole collection is open. Download the full dataset as
    <a href={`${base}downloads/usccb_movie_archive.sqlite`}>SQLite</a> or
    <a href={`${base}downloads/usccb_movie_archive.csv`}>CSV</a>, or query it live as
    JSON: there's a read-only
    <a href="https://github.com/arathalion/USCCB-Movie-Archive/blob/main/docs/api.md">public API</a>
    (Supabase&nbsp;PostgREST) with filtering, sorting, full-text search, and genres —
    no signup required. The site itself runs on the same backend.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    Notes on the data: <code>year</code> is missing for ~3.6% of films and <code>mpaa_rating</code>
    for ~42% (mostly pre-1968 and foreign titles). Full reviews exist for 1,619 films; the rest
    have a short synopsis.
  </p>
</Base>
